# vortex: the ornament is the route
#
#     $ vonal run examples/vortex.vsr
#     vortex
#
# Every other plate decorates with cells the eye never reaches. Here there are
# none: the eye starts at (0,0), runs every one of the 225 cells exactly once,
# and halts on the void at the centre. The picture is a diagram of its own
# control flow.
#
# The route is a square spiral. Each ring is walked clockwise, and one step
# short of closing it, the triangle at (k, k+1) points east instead of north,
# which drops the eye onto the next ring in. Those seven cells are the seam
# running down the diagonal from the top left.
#
# Most of the triangles do nothing. A turn that restates the heading the eye
# already has costs a step and changes nothing, so a whole side of a ring can
# be triangles and still be a straight run. Only the corners and the seam
# actually turn. That is also why a cell on a straight run is free to be any
# instruction that does not turn: the first few on each ring are discs and a
# cross that spell one character in base 8 and print it, so the seven rings
# print the plate's own name, outermost first.
#
# Every turn is variant 1, the unconditional one. Variants 2 and 3 would pop
# the stack, which is empty, so the triangles share one figure offset, and
# the colour comes from the ground instead: one band per ring, the figures
# following it. The triangles shrink toward the centre (scale 7 - ring),
# which is free because a turn never reads its scale. So is the cross's.
# The discs' sizes are the digits.

%plate 15x15

o11.  o26.  o26.  +27.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  >17.  v17.
>17.  o117  o257  o277  +267  >167  >167  >167  >167  >167  >167  >167  >167  v167  v17.
^17.  >167  o112  o262  o222  +252  >152  >152  >152  >152  >152  >152  v152  v167  v17.
^17.  ^167  >152  o113  o263  o243  +243  >143  >143  >143  >143  v143  v152  v167  v17.
^17.  ^167  ^152  >143  o114  o244  o254  +234  >134  >134  v134  v143  v152  v167  v17.
